course: dictionary
lesson: 2180
title: Cortisol, the stress hormone
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Cortisol is a hormone, a chemical messenger that your body makes. It helps your body deal with stress, and it also helps control your energy, your sleep and when you wake up.
explain: In science lessons, cortisol is often called «the stress hormone». It rises when you are scared or worried, which helps your body react, and then it should fall again.
explain: «Dreading something can make the body release the stress hormone cortisol beforehand, and a sore tummy is a common sign of that worry.» The clues are dreading, worry and stress. Dreading means feeling worried about something before it happens, so cortisol rises before the event.
explain: Cortisol is normally highest in the morning, which helps you wake up. Sleep, exercise and calm breathing help keep it healthy.
fact: A hormone is a chemical messenger made by the body.
fact: Cortisol is called «the stress hormone» in science and health lessons.
fact: Cortisol usually rises when you are scared or worried, and it should fall again afterwards.
fact: Cortisol is normally highest in the morning, which helps you wake up.
fact: Sleep, exercise and calm breathing help keep cortisol healthy.
exercise: «Dreading something can make the body release the stress hormone cortisol beforehand.» What is cortisol? | A bone in the arm that helps you move | A machine that measures the heart | A sweet food the body needs to grow | *A hormone, a chemical messenger made by the body
exercise: In science lessons, cortisol is often called what? | the sleep bone | the morning cloud | *the stress hormone | the happy sugar
exercise: According to the lesson, what makes cortisol rise? | Eating a big dinner | Reading a quiet book | *Being scared or worried | Counting to ten slowly
test: In the sentence, what sign of worry is given? | A bright smile | *A sore tummy | A long nap | A new pair of shoes
test: When is cortisol normally highest? | After a long swim | At bedtime | In the middle of the night | *In the morning
exam: Which of these helps keep cortisol healthy? | Eating sweets all day and never sleeping | Staying up all night worrying | *Sleep, exercise and calm breathing | Sitting still and never breathing deeply
